window.CLATMOCK={"n":2,"title":"Mock Test 2","theme":"Current affairs mix","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Core subjects with passages built on recent Supreme Court and High Court developments, from constitutional law to company, tax and competition law.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"p":[{"id":"P01","s":"Jurisprudence","sf":"Jurisprudence","t":"Natural Law: From Aquinas to Radbruch and Finnis","x":"The natural law tradition holds that there is some necessary connection between law and morality. Thomas Aquinas distinguished four kinds of law: eternal law, the divine reason governing the universe; natural law, the participation of rational creatures in the eternal law; divine law, revealed in scripture; and human law, which is derived from natural law either as a conclusion from its precepts or as a determination of matters it leaves open. Aquinas said that an unjust human law 'seems not to be law at all' but a perversion of law; he added, however, that such a law may nonetheless bind in conscience where disobedience would cause scandal or disturbance, a qualification showing that his thesis concerns the full or focal sense of law rather than its mere social existence. John Finnis, in Natural Law and Natural Rights (1980), revived the tradition without deriving morality from metaphysics. He identifies basic goods, self-evident to practical reason, such as life, knowledge, play, aesthetic experience, sociability, practical reasonableness and religion, and argues that law should be understood by its 'central case', the law of a community that effectively secures the common good with fairness. Law that fails this standard is not non-law, but a 'peripheral' case, defective as law and lacking the moral claim to obedience. Gustav Radbruch, after the Second World War, argued that where the conflict between positive law and justice reaches an intolerable degree, the statute, as 'flawed law', must yield to justice; ordinary injustice does not deprive a statute of validity, but extreme injustice does. H.L.A. Hart, a leading positivist, accepted a 'minimum content of natural law': given the human vulnerability to attack, approximate equality, limited altruism, limited resources and limited understanding and strength of will, any social order that is to survive must contain rules restraining violence, regulating property and requiring promises to be kept. Hart insisted, however, that these are contingent truisms about human nature and not a moral test of the validity of particular laws.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following best captures Aquinas's position on an unjust human law, as presented in the passage?","o":["It is not law in the full sense, yet may bind in conscience to avoid scandal","It remains law where it can be derived from divine law, and otherwise lacks force","It is fully law, binding in conscience and in practice, even if its moral content is poor","It is valid law, but one that a rational person ought generally to disobey"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records both the 'perversion of law' claim and the qualification that the law may bind in conscience to avoid scandal; the thesis concerns the focal sense of law.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to Finnis, a legal system that persistently fails to secure the common good with fairness is","o":["the central case of law, since it still has legal form and effective enforcement","not a legal system at all, since law without fairness has no social existence","valid and morally binding, because effective enforcement creates the duty to obey","a peripheral case of law, defective as law and lacking moral claim to obedience"],"a":3,"e":"Finnis's 'central case' method treats failing systems as peripheral, not non-existent.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under the Radbruch formula as stated in the passage, which of the following statutes would most clearly lose the character of law?","o":["A statute that retrospectively raises the penalty for a minor regulatory offence","A statute imposing a harsh and unpopular tax that is arguably justifiable on revenue grounds","A statute whose drafting error produces an unfair result in some cases","A statute denying a racial group all legal personality and authorising its extermination"],"a":3,"e":"Only extreme injustice, intolerable in degree, defeats validity; ordinary injustice does not.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Hart's 'minimum content of natural law' is best understood as","o":["a rejection of the rule of recognition as the ultimate test of validity in a modern legal system","proof that law and morality are necessarily identical in their content","contingent facts about human nature that explain why viable legal orders share basic rules","a moral test that every valid law must pass in order to count as law"],"a":2,"e":"The passage says Hart regarded these as contingent truisms and not a moral test of validity.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following statements about Finnis is INCORRECT on the basis of the passage?","o":["He revived natural law without deriving morality from metaphysics","He rejects the 'central case' method and treats all legal systems alike","He includes practical reasonableness and religion among the basic goods","He regards the basic goods as self-evident to practical reason itself"],"a":1,"e":"Finnis's whole account of law relies on the central case; the other statements are accurate.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Assertion (A): Hart's acceptance of a 'minimum content of natural law' does not make him a natural lawyer. Reason (R): Hart treats the minimum content as contingent facts about human nature, not as a criterion of legal validity. Which is correct?","o":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"a":2,"e":"The natural-law thesis concerns a necessary moral test of validity. Hart denies that; his minimum content is only a set of contingent facts, which is precisely why he remains a positivist.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P02","s":"Jurisprudence","sf":"Jurisprudence","t":"Hohfeld's Jural Relations and the Question of Legal Personality","x":"Wesley Hohfeld argued that the word 'right' is used loosely for four different legal relations, and set out eight fundamental jural conceptions in two groups of correlatives and opposites. A claim-right corresponds to a duty in another: if A has a right that B pay him, B has a duty to pay. A privilege (or liberty) is the absence of a duty and has as its correlative a 'no-right' in others: A's privilege to enter his own land means that others have no right that he refrain from doing so. A power is the ability to alter legal relations, and its correlative is a liability: the promisee's power to accept an offer subjects the offeror to a liability. An immunity is freedom from the legal power of another to alter one's legal position, and its correlative is a disability. The opposites are, respectively, no-right, duty, disability and liability. Hohfeld's scheme is often applied to questions of legal personality, the capacity to bear rights and duties. A natural person is a legal person, but the law also recognises artificial or juristic persons such as corporations, idols and, in some systems, rivers. The fiction theory of Savigny treats such entities as creations of the law's imagination, whereas the realist theory of Gierke treats them as real groups with a will of their own. In Yogendra Nath Naskar v. CIT (1969), the Supreme Court held that a Hindu idol is a juristic person, with the shebait acting as manager, and in Shiromani Gurdwara Prabandhak Committee v. Som Nath Dass (2000), the same status was accorded to the Guru Granth Sahib. In 2017, the Uttarakhand High Court in Mohd. Salim v. State of Uttarakhand declared the Ganga and Yamuna to be living legal persons, in the manner of New Zealand's recognition of the Whanganui River, but the Supreme Court stayed that decision. In Animal Welfare Board of India v. A. Nagaraja (2014), the Court, without conferring personality, read the rights of animals into Article 21 as an expansion of the concept of life.","q":[{"q":"In Hohfeld's scheme, the correlative of a 'power' is","o":["a no-right","a duty","a disability","a liability"],"a":3,"e":"Power corresponds to liability; immunity corresponds to disability.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Parliament cannot amend the basic structure of the Constitution. In Hohfeldian terms this is best described as","o":["a power of the Court to amend the Constitution in place of Parliament","a privilege of Parliament, with a corresponding no-right in the citizen","a disability of Parliament, correlating to an immunity of the basic structure","a liability of Parliament, correlating to a power of the Court to amend"],"a":2,"e":"Absence of power in Parliament is a disability; its correlative is the immunity of the other party, here the protected basic features.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The owner of land enters it freely, and no one else can complain. The Hohfeldian relation and its correlative are","o":["power and liability","immunity and disability","privilege and no-right","claim-right and duty"],"a":2,"e":"Freedom from duty to stay out is a privilege; its correlative is the no-right of others.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is correct on the basis of the passage?","o":["The Supreme Court upheld the High Court's declaration that the Ganga and Yamuna are legal persons as in New Zealand","Animal Welfare Board v. Nagaraja conferred legal personality on animals under Article 21","Gierke's theory treats juristic persons as mere fictions created by the law","The Supreme Court recognised a Hindu idol as a juristic person but stayed the Ganga-Yamuna declaration"],"a":3,"e":"Naskar upheld idol personality; the Mohd. Salim decision was stayed; Nagaraja did not confer personality; Gierke's theory is the realist view.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A promisee holds a power to accept an offer within a stipulated time. Before acceptance, the offeror's corresponding position is","o":["an immunity","a duty","a liability","a disability"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that the correlative of power is liability.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The best difference between the fiction theory and the realist theory of juristic personality is that the fiction theory","o":["applies to corporations, whereas the realist theory is confined to idols and rivers","treats the group as a real entity with a will, whereas the realist theory treats it as a fiction","denies that corporations can hold rights, whereas the realist theory permits them","treats personality as a creation of law, whereas the realist theory sees a real group will"],"a":3,"e":"Savigny sees personality as a legal fiction; Gierke sees real group personality.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P03","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"The Right to Die with Dignity: Harish Rana v. Union of India (2026)","x":"Article 21 guarantees that no person shall be deprived of life or personal liberty except according to procedure established by law. In Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab (1996), a Constitution Bench held that the right to life does not include the right to die, and upheld the penal provision against abetment of suicide, but observed that a dying person in a terminal state or a persistent vegetative state may be allowed to die a natural death, since the right to live with dignity includes the right to die with dignity at the end of life. In Aruna Shanbaug v. Union of India (2011), the Court permitted passive euthanasia, the withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment, under High Court supervision, while distinguishing it from active euthanasia, which involves a positive act to end life and remains unlawful. In Common Cause v. Union of India (2018), a Constitution Bench recognised the right to die with dignity as part of Article 21, upheld advance medical directives, or 'living wills', and laid down a procedure involving medical boards; the procedure was simplified by the Court in January 2023. In Harish Rana v. Union of India, decided on 11 March 2026 by a Bench of Justices J.B. Pardiwala and K.V. Viswanathan, the Supreme Court gave effect for the first time to this framework in an individual case. Harish Rana, who was injured in a fall in 2013, had remained in a persistent vegetative state for about thirteen years, and his parents sought permission for the withdrawal of treatment. The Delhi High Court had declined relief on the ground that he was not on mechanical life support and was able to sustain himself. The Supreme Court rejected that distinction, treating clinically assisted nutrition and hydration as a medical intervention, and permitted its withdrawal under medical supervision, on the basis of the opinions of medical boards and the best interests of the patient. The Court also waived the usual reconsideration period in the case, and observed that the matter did not create a new doctrine, but applied principles laid down in 2011, 2018 and 2023.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following correctly states the holding in Gian Kaur as described in the passage?","o":["The right to life includes a right to die by personal choice in most end-of-life situations","Passive euthanasia is permitted in most cases, with few safeguards required","There is no right to die, though dying with dignity in terminal states is protected","The provision penalising abetment of suicide is unconstitutional and was struck down"],"a":2,"e":"The Court rejected a right to die but observed that dying with dignity in terminal states is part of the right to live with dignity.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The distinction between active and passive euthanasia, as drawn in Aruna Shanbaug, is that","o":["passive euthanasia withdraws life-sustaining treatment and is permitted; active euthanasia remains unlawful","both are lawful where the patient is terminally ill and a medical board has certified the condition","both are unlawful, and the difference matters mainly for the sentence imposed on the doctor","active euthanasia is lawful where the family consents, while passive euthanasia is unlawful"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records the Court's distinction.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The reasoning of the Delhi High Court rejected by the Supreme Court in Harish Rana was that","o":["no medical board had been constituted to examine the patient's condition","the parents had no standing to seek withdrawal of treatment on his behalf","passive euthanasia is not recognised in India without a statute from Parliament","he was not on mechanical life support and could sustain himself, so relief was refused"],"a":3,"e":"The Supreme Court rejected the distinction based on absence of mechanical life support.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In the Supreme Court's reasoning in Harish Rana, clinically assisted nutrition and hydration was treated as","o":["a form of active euthanasia, and so unlawful even under the framework","an unconstitutional practice for which no relief could be granted","ordinary basic care that generally cannot be withdrawn even on medical advice","a medical intervention that may be withdrawn under the framework"],"a":3,"e":"The Court treated it as a medical intervention, which brought withdrawal within passive euthanasia.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is correct?","o":["Harish Rana was the first individual case to apply the Common Cause framework","Harish Rana created a largely new doctrine unconnected with earlier cases","Aruna Shanbaug permitted active euthanasia under High Court supervision","Common Cause rejected living wills but simplified the medical board procedure"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records the application of the earlier principles and the first-time effect in an individual case.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Consider the following. 1. The Court in Common Cause recognised the right to die with dignity as part of Article 21. 2. The Court in Common Cause rejected advance medical directives. 3. The procedure of Common Cause was simplified in January 2023. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"e":"Statements 1 and 3 appear in the passage; statement 2 is wrong because living wills were upheld.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P04","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"Menstrual Health as a Fundamental Right: Dr. Jaya Thakur v. Union of India (2026)","x":"Article 21A, inserted by the 86th Amendment in 2002, provides for free and compulsory education to children between six and fourteen years, and the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009 (RTE Act) gives effect to it. Article 14 has been interpreted to guarantee not only formal equality but substantive equality, which requires the State to take account of structural disadvantage. In K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017), the Supreme Court held that dignity, bodily autonomy and privacy are facets of the right to life. In Dr. Jaya Thakur v. Union of India, decided on 30 January 2026 by a Bench of Justices J.B. Pardiwala and R. Mahadevan, the Supreme Court considered a petition under Article 32 seeking free sanitary napkins and facilities for girls in schools. The Court held that the right to life under Article 21 includes the right to menstrual health, since a life with dignity cannot be realised without privacy and bodily autonomy, and that inadequate access to menstrual hygiene management is a barrier to education which violates Article 21A and the RTE Act, as well as the principle of substantive equality under Article 14. The Court issued binding directions to all States and Union Territories to provide free biodegradable sanitary napkins to girl students, to ensure that every school has functional, hygienic, gender-segregated toilets with water, and facilities accessible to children with disabilities, and to designate officers to monitor compliance. It warned that private schools which fail to comply risk the withdrawal of recognition under the RTE Act. In September 2026, expressing dissatisfaction with compliance reports from several States, the Court directed District Education Officers to constitute teams of teachers to conduct surprise inspections of schools. The judgment is cited as an example of the Court's positive-obligation approach, under which the State must act to remove obstacles that prevent the realisation of rights.","q":[{"q":"The Court in Dr. Jaya Thakur held menstrual health to be a facet of","o":["Article 19(1)(g), as part of the freedom to carry on an occupation","Article 21 alone, with no consequence for Article 21A or Article 14","the Directive Principles, and therefore not enforceable under Article 32","Article 21, with its denial violating Article 21A, the RTE Act and Article 14"],"a":3,"e":"The passage records all these constitutional foundations.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The passage describes 'substantive equality' as the idea that","o":["identical treatment of persons even where their circumstances differ","equality confined to economic and fiscal matters under Article 14","equality as an aspirational Directive Principle that may not be enforced in court","the State must account for structural disadvantage in securing equality"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that Article 14 includes substantive equality, which requires the State to consider structural disadvantage.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT among the directions mentioned in the passage?","o":["Mandatory monthly medical examination of every girl student","Facilities accessible to children with disabilities in every school","Free biodegradable sanitary napkins distributed to girl students","Functional, gender-segregated toilets with water in every school"],"a":0,"e":"The last is not mentioned; the others are directions in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A private unaided school refuses to install the facilities directed by the Court. The consequence indicated in the passage is","o":["a monetary penalty imposed on the management by the Court itself","prosecution of the school's managers under the criminal law","a risk of having its recognition withdrawn under the RTE Act","no consequence, as the directions bind mainly government schools"],"a":2,"e":"The Court warned of de-recognition.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The 'positive-obligation approach' mentioned in the passage means that","o":["courts may not issue directions to States on matters of policy","the State's duty is to refrain from interfering with individual rights","private parties, rather than the State, owe duties towards realisation of rights","the State must act to remove obstacles to the realisation of rights"],"a":3,"e":"The passage describes the approach as requiring the State to act to remove obstacles.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Assertion (A): The Court treated lack of menstrual hygiene facilities as a violation of Article 21A. Reason (R): Lack of such facilities causes absenteeism and drop-out, which acts as a barrier to free and compulsory education. Which is correct?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R explains A","Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"The Court linked the barrier to education with the violation of Article 21A and the RTE Act.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P05","s":"Constitutional Law","sf":"Constitutional Law","t":"The 'Creamy Layer' and Parental Employment: Union of India v. Rohith Nathan (2026)","x":"In Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992), the Supreme Court upheld reservation of 27 per cent for Other Backward Classes under Article 16(4) but held that the 'creamy layer', the socially advanced among them, must be excluded. The Government implemented this through the Office Memorandum of 8 September 1993, which sets out categories of persons whose children are not entitled to the OBC benefit. These include constitutional office-holders and those in specified service categories, such as the children of Group A or Class I officers, and, under a residual category, persons whose family income from sources other than salary and agriculture exceeds a specified amount, now Rs 8 lakh per annum, subject to periodic revision. Salary and agricultural income are, under the memorandum, excluded from this income test, since the status of the service held by the parents is the principal criterion for salaried persons. A 2004 letter from the Department of Personnel and Training (DoPT) stated that, where equivalence between posts in public sector undertakings or private employment and government posts had not been established, the salary income of such parents would be taken into account under the income test. In Union of India v. Rohith Nathan, decided on 11 March 2026 by Justices P.S. Narasimha and R. Mahadevan, the Supreme Court held that this practice, which was applied to candidates in the Civil Services Examination, was unsustainable in law. The Court held that the creamy layer is determined by social status and not by income alone, that an executive letter of clarification cannot add a substantive condition absent from the parent policy, and that treating the children of PSU or private employees differently from the children of government employees amounts to hostile discrimination under Articles 14 and 16. Pending the determination of equivalence, only the income test excluding salary would apply to such candidates. The Court directed the authorities to issue a fresh memorandum and to accommodate candidates wrongly denied the benefit, including through supernumerary posts. In November 2026, the Court reaffirmed its decision, refusing the Union's plea that it operate only prospectively, while clarifying that it does not reopen concluded selections, though it applies to the allocation of services under the Civil Services Examination, 2025.","q":[{"q":"The principal ground on which the 2004 DoPT letter was held unsustainable was that","o":["it was issued by an officer below the rank competent to issue memoranda under the 1993 scheme","it applied to government employees, and so left PSU staff outside the policy","it added a substantive condition by executive letter and created hostile discrimination","it had not been published in the Gazette and so could not bind candidates"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the letter introduced a substantive condition and created discrimination under Articles 14 and 16.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under the 1993 Office Memorandum as described, salary income of parents is","o":["included in the income test for most categories, along with agricultural income","the main criterion for the creamy layer, ahead of the service held by the parent","excluded from the income test, as service status is the primary criterion","irrelevant to the creamy layer for most categories of candidates"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that salary and agricultural income are excluded from the income test.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Court held that, pending determination of equivalence, the position of PSU and private-sector parents is that","o":["only the income test, excluding salary income, is applied to them","they are excluded from reservation until equivalence is determined","they are treated as in the creamy layer until equivalence is found","the Government may decide their status on a case-by-case basis"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states this directly.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Court's refusal of prospective operation in November 2026 means that","o":["the decision is set aside and the Union's plea for prospective effect is allowed","it applies to the 2025 Civil Services allocation, but concluded selections stay closed","the decision is confined to examinations held after the judgment","the decision reopens concluded selections under earlier examinations and orders re-allocation"],"a":1,"e":"The passage describes the clarification.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Consider the following statements. 1. Indra Sawhney upheld 27 per cent reservation for OBCs and required exclusion of the creamy layer. 2. The Court in Rohith Nathan held that creamy layer is determined solely by income. 3. The Court directed fresh memorandum and accommodation of wrongly denied candidates. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"e":"Statement 2 is false: the Court held that status and not income alone is the test.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The decision in Rohith Nathan is best described as an application of the principle that","o":["executive instructions may override a settled policy in the interest of administration","the creamy layer exclusion is itself invalid for public employment under Article 16","similarly placed persons must be treated alike, and instructions must follow the policy","income is the main constitutional test of backwardness for the creamy layer under Article 16(4)"],"a":2,"e":"The decision draws on Articles 14 and 16 and the limits of executive clarification.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P06","s":"Criminal Procedure","sf":"Criminal Procedure","t":"Anticipatory Bail, Default Bail and the Limits of Custodial Investigation","x":"Section 438 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, now Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (BNSS), empowers the High Court and the Court of Session to grant anticipatory bail to a person who apprehends arrest for a non-bailable offence. In Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia v. State of Punjab (1980), a Constitution Bench held that the power is not to be hedged by rigid conditions, such as a time limit, and must be exercised in the light of the facts of each case, in aid of Article 21. In Sushila Aggarwal v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2020), another Constitution Bench held that anticipatory bail need not be limited in time and may continue until the end of the trial, unless the court, for reasons, limits it, and that it does not automatically end upon the summoning of the accused or the filing of the charge-sheet. Default bail is a different right. Under the proviso to Section 167(2) CrPC (now Section 187 BNSS), if the investigation is not completed within sixty or ninety days, depending upon the offence, the accused 'shall be released on bail', and the right is considered indefeasible. In Uday Mohanlal Acharya v. State of Maharashtra (2001), the Court held that the right must be exercised by the accused before the charge-sheet is filed, and cannot be claimed once the charge-sheet has been filed. In Ritu Chhabaria v. Union of India (2023), the Court held that the investigating agency cannot defeat default bail by filing an incomplete charge-sheet, or a 'preliminary' one, while the investigation is continuing, and that further investigation under Section 173(8) is not a licence to withhold the completed charge-sheet. In Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar (2014), the Court directed that, in offences punishable with imprisonment up to seven years, arrest should not be automatic, and that the police should issue a notice of appearance, giving reasons for arrest, a safeguard now embedded in the BNSS. In Satender Kumar Antil v. CBI (2022), the Court classified offences for the grant of bail, and urged the legislature to consider a separate Bail Act.","q":[{"q":"The holding in Sushila Aggarwal on the duration of anticipatory bail is that it","o":["may continue until the end of the trial unless the court limits it for reasons","ends automatically on the filing of the charge-sheet in the case","must be limited to a fixed number of days fixed by the court","ends automatically on the summoning of the accused by the court"],"a":0,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Default bail under Section 167(2) is described as indefeasible, which means that","o":["it is lost if the accused does not apply within a day of the deadline","the court has a wide discretion to refuse it even after the period expires and no charge-sheet is filed","it arises when investigation is not completed in time, and must be claimed before charge-sheet","it can be claimed at any stage, even after the charge-sheet has been filed"],"a":2,"e":"Uday Mohanlal Acharya requires the accused to apply before the charge-sheet is filed.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The investigating agency files an unsigned, incomplete 'preliminary' charge-sheet on the 89th day in a case where the period is ninety days, to avoid default bail. According to Ritu Chhabaria, this","o":["defeats the right to default bail, since a charge-sheet was filed in time","cannot defeat the right to default bail, as the investigation is incomplete","makes the arrest illegal ab initio and may entitle the accused to compensation","leaves the court with no power to grant bail until the charge-sheet is completed"],"a":1,"e":"An incomplete charge-sheet cannot be used to defeat default bail.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"In Arnesh Kumar, the Court's direction concerned offences punishable with imprisonment","o":["up to ten years","up to three years","for life","up to seven years"],"a":3,"e":"The passage specifies seven years.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following statements is correct?","o":["Default bail is available mainly in cases of offences punishable by death","Anticipatory bail is a species of default bail available after the charge-sheet","Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia held that anticipatory bail must generally be limited in time","Satender Kumar Antil urged the legislature to consider a separate Bail Act"],"a":3,"e":"The passage records the Antil suggestion; the other statements are incorrect.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Consider the following. 1. Anticipatory bail is granted to a person who apprehends arrest. 2. Default bail arises from the failure to complete investigation within the statutory time. 3. Anticipatory bail and default bail are the same remedy. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"The passage treats them as distinct remedies.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P07","s":"Criminal Law","sf":"Criminal Law","t":"Consent, Marital Exception and the Law of Sexual Offences","x":"Section 63 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (BNS) defines rape, replacing Section 375 of the Indian Penal Code. A man commits rape if he has sexual intercourse with a woman in any of seven descriptions, including against her will, without her consent, with consent obtained by putting her in fear of death or hurt, with consent given under a misconception that he is her husband, or when she is under eighteen years of age, with or without her consent. The Explanation states that consent means an unequivocal voluntary agreement, communicated by words, gestures or any form of verbal or non-verbal communication, though the absence of physical resistance does not by itself imply consent. Exception 2 provides that sexual intercourse by a man with his own wife, the wife not being under eighteen years of age, is not rape. In Independent Thought v. Union of India (2017), the Supreme Court read down the exception, which at the time referred to a wife under fifteen, so that intercourse with a wife under eighteen is rape, in order to harmonise it with the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012 and the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act. The Court declined to decide on the exception for adult wives. A challenge to the marital exception for adult wives, brought on the ground of Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21, resulted in a split verdict in the Delhi High Court in 2022, and an appeal has been pending before the Supreme Court; the Union has argued that the question involves social and family considerations best left to Parliament and that other remedies exist, including for cruelty. Section 69 of the BNS separately penalises sexual intercourse by deceitful means, including a false promise to marry, with imprisonment up to ten years; the Supreme Court has held, in decisions such as Pramod Suryabhan Pawar v. State of Maharashtra (2019), that a breach of promise to marry is not a false promise unless the promise was made with no intention of keeping it at the outset and was the cause of the consent. The age of consent is eighteen years, as the law stands.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the descriptions of rape in the passage?","o":["With consent, when she is a woman over twenty-one years of age","Against her will","When she is under eighteen, with or without her consent","With consent obtained by putting her in fear of death or hurt"],"a":0,"e":"The passage lists the other three; consent of an adult woman, validly given, does not constitute rape.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Independent Thought, the Supreme Court","o":["struck down Exception 2 entirely, as violating Articles 14, 15 and 21, for all wives","upheld the exception as it stood, for adult and minor wives alike","read down the exception so that intercourse with a wife under eighteen is rape","held that marital rape is an offence in respect of all wives"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The status of the challenge to the marital exception for adult wives is that","o":["a split verdict in the Delhi High Court led to a pending appeal in the Supreme Court","it was dismissed by Parliament after a detailed legislative debate","it was withdrawn by the petitioners before the Supreme Court could hear it","it was decided by the Supreme Court and the exception was struck down"],"a":0,"e":"The passage describes the split verdict and the pending appeal.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A man promises to marry a woman, sincerely intending to do so, has intercourse with her with her consent, and later changes his mind. According to Pramod Suryabhan Pawar as described, this is","o":["rape under Section 63, since the consent was obtained by a promise","not an offence under Section 69, as the promise was not false at the outset","an offence under Section 69, because the promise was ultimately broken","an offence of cheating by default, since the promise was later broken"],"a":1,"e":"A breach of promise is not a false promise unless there was no intention at the outset.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Under the Explanation to Section 63, the absence of physical resistance","o":["does not by itself imply consent","implies that consent was later withdrawn","amounts to consent in the circumstances","is the main test for deciding consent"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states that the absence of resistance does not by itself imply consent.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Assertion (A): The presence of consent of a girl aged seventeen is irrelevant to the offence of rape. Reason (R): The BNS treats sexual intercourse with a woman under eighteen as rape, with or without her consent. Which is correct?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R explains A","A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A"],"a":0,"e":"The age limit makes consent irrelevant.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P08","s":"Environmental Law","sf":"Environmental Law","t":"Principles of Environmental Jurisprudence and the Meaning of 'Forest'","x":"Indian environmental jurisprudence has been built largely by the Supreme Court through Article 21, which includes the right to a pollution-free environment (Subhash Kumar v. State of Bihar, 1991), read with Article 48A, which directs the State to protect the environment, and Article 51A(g), which makes protection of the environment a fundamental duty. In Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v. Union of India (1996), the Court held that the 'precautionary principle' and the 'polluter pays' principle are part of the law of the land, as essential features of sustainable development, and that the burden of proving that an activity is environmentally benign lies on the person proposing it. In Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v. Union of India (1996), the Court held that the polluter is liable not only to compensate the victims but also to bear the cost of restoring the environment, and relied on the absolute liability principle. In M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath (1997), the Court applied the 'public trust doctrine', holding that the State holds natural resources such as rivers, forests and the seashore as a trustee for the public, and cannot allocate them to private owners for commercial use in disregard of this trust. In T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad v. Union of India (1996), the Court held that the word 'forest' in the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 must be understood according to its dictionary meaning, so as to include all areas recorded as forest in government records, whatever the nature of the ownership, as well as areas which answer the description of forest. In 2023, Parliament amended the Act, renaming it the Van (Sanrakshan Evam Samvardhan) Adhiniyam, and limited its application to land notified as forest under the Indian Forest Act, or recorded as forest in government records on or after 25 October 1980, while exempting certain categories, such as linear projects within a limited distance of borders and security-related infrastructure. Challenges were made on the ground that the amendment narrowed the Godavarman definition, and in February 2024 the Supreme Court directed, as an interim measure, that the dictionary meaning of forest should continue to apply and that the State authorities should compile records of forest-like areas. The National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 confers on the Tribunal jurisdiction over substantial questions relating to the environment and enables the award of relief and compensation.","q":[{"q":"Under Vellore Citizens, the burden of proving that an activity is environmentally benign lies on","o":["the person challenging the activity before the Tribunal","the person proposing the activity","the Government, in most cases where the activity is licensed","the Tribunal, which must investigate on its own initiative"],"a":1,"e":"The Court reversed the burden of proof in line with the precautionary principle.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The 'public trust doctrine' as applied in M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath means that","o":["private persons may own rivers, forests and the seashore outright","the State owns natural resources outright and may allocate them as it wishes to private persons","the Union, rather than the State, may regulate natural resources","the State holds natural resources as trustee for the public, not for private commercial use"],"a":3,"e":"This is the doctrine as stated in the passage.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Godavarman decision interpreted 'forest' in the 1980 Act to mean","o":["the dictionary meaning, including areas recorded as forests regardless of ownership","only areas that are declared as national parks, sanctuaries or tiger reserves under law","areas that are declared as national parks or sanctuaries","only reserved forests recorded as such before 1980"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the dictionary meaning approach.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"The 2023 amendment restricted the Act to land","o":["of any ecological value, whether or not recorded as forest","notified or recorded as forest on or after 25 October 1980, with some exemptions","situated above a specified altitude in the Himalayan region","owned by the Government, whether or not it was recorded as forest or notified at any time"],"a":1,"e":"The passage describes the narrowed scope.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The interim direction of the Supreme Court in February 2024 was that","o":["the dictionary meaning should continue to apply and States should compile forest records","the 2023 amendment is void and the 1980 Act is restored in its original form","the Act applies mainly to notified forests until the pending hearing is complete","the Godavarman case is overruled and the new definition is to be applied"],"a":0,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A company proposes a mining project with uncertain ecological impact. On the principles in the passage, the company","o":["can be held liable mainly for the cost of compensating victims after the harm","is exempt, because a commercial mining project with State approval serves a larger public purpose","must show the project is environmentally benign, and pay restoration costs if it pollutes","need not prove anything until harm occurs, since the burden lies on challengers"],"a":2,"e":"Precautionary and polluter pays principles together impose the burden and restoration costs.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P09","s":"Company Law","sf":"Company Law","t":"Corporate Personality, Minority Protection and the Tata-Mistry Dispute","x":"In Salomon v. Salomon & Co. (1897), the House of Lords held that a company, once incorporated, is a legal person distinct from its members, even if one member owns almost all the shares. The corporate veil may, however, be lifted in exceptional cases, such as fraud, sham arrangements to avoid existing obligations (Gilford Motor Co. v. Horne; Jones v. Lipman), or in tax evasion and trading with the enemy. In Life Insurance Corporation of India v. Escorts Ltd. (1986), the Supreme Court said that the veil may be lifted where the statute itself so contemplates, or where fraud or improper conduct is sought to be prevented. In Vodafone International Holdings v. Union of India (2012), the Court held that, in the absence of a sham or colourable device, the corporate structure of a transaction must be respected for tax purposes. The rule in Foss v. Harbottle provides that the proper plaintiff for a wrong done to the company is the company itself, and that the courts will not interfere with internal management where the majority can ratify an irregularity, subject to exceptions for fraud on the minority, ultra vires acts and acts requiring a special majority. Sections 241 and 242 of the Companies Act, 2013 allow members to apply to the National Company Law Tribunal where the affairs of the company are being conducted in a manner prejudicial to the public interest or oppressive to any member, or where there is a material change in management prejudicial to interests of the company; the Tribunal must be of the opinion that winding up would unfairly prejudice the members but that the facts justify a winding-up order on 'just and equitable' grounds. In Tata Sons Ltd. v. Cyrus Investments Pvt. Ltd. (2021), the Supreme Court set aside the NCLAT's order reinstating Cyrus Mistry as Executive Chairman, holding that the removal of a director, even a chairman, was not oppressive, that the Articles of Association are a contract binding the members, and that the NCLAT had no power to reinstate where relief was not sought and no case of winding-up on just and equitable grounds was made out. Section 166 of the 2013 Act codifies directors' duties, requiring good faith and acting in the best interests of the company, its employees, shareholders, community and environment.","q":[{"q":"The ratio of Salomon v. Salomon & Co. is that","o":["a company is a separate legal person even where one member holds nearly all the shares","a company is the agent of its members and generally bound by their acts","a company can have no liability for the acts of its members","a company controlled by one member is not validly incorporated"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the separate-personality principle.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT identified in the passage as a ground for lifting the corporate veil?","o":["Fraud","Statutory provision itself","Mere inconvenience to the creditors","Sham arrangements to avoid existing obligations"],"a":2,"e":"Mere inconvenience of creditors is not a ground.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The exceptions to the rule in Foss v. Harbottle include","o":["acts of directors that are done in bad faith or without authority","any act which the minority dislikes or finds unfair to its own interests in the company","only ultra vires acts of the company","fraud on the minority, ultra vires acts and acts requiring a special majority"],"a":3,"e":"The passage lists these exceptions.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Tata Sons v. Cyrus Investments, the Supreme Court held that","o":["directors cannot be removed from office once appointed, unless the members unanimously agree","the removal of Mr. Mistry was oppressive and he must be reinstated as Executive Chairman, as the NCLAT ordered","the removal was not oppressive, the Articles bind the members, and reinstatement was unavailable","private companies cannot be made subject to Section 241 proceedings"],"a":2,"e":"The passage sets out the holding.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Under Sections 241-242 as described, before the Tribunal makes an order the Tribunal must opine that","o":["the company has no assets remaining to meet its liabilities","winding up would unfairly prejudice the members, though just and equitable grounds exist","the majority is dishonest and has acted in bad faith","the company is insolvent and cannot pay its debts as they fall due to its secured and unsecured creditors"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states this requirement.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A shareholder believes the directors committed a wrong against the company. Under the rule in Foss v. Harbottle, the proper plaintiff is","o":["the Registrar of Companies, acting on a complaint","the company, subject to exceptions such as fraud on the minority","the auditor, who must file on the company's behalf","the shareholder, in most cases where the company is wronged"],"a":1,"e":"The proper plaintiff rule favours the company, subject to exceptions.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P10","s":"Tax Law","sf":"Tax Law","t":"Goods and Services Tax: Constitutional Scheme, Council and Credit","x":"The Constitution (101st Amendment) Act, 2016 introduced Article 246A, which empowers both Parliament and State Legislatures to make laws on goods and services tax, with Parliament having exclusive power for inter-State supply, Article 269A for inter-State levy and apportionment, and Article 279A, which establishes the GST Council, composed of the Union Finance Minister, the Union Minister of State in charge of revenue, and State Ministers in charge of finance. The Council's recommendations on rates, exemptions and thresholds are made by a three-fourths majority of the weighted votes of members present and voting, with the Centre holding one-third of the weight and States together two-thirds. In Union of India v. Mohit Minerals (2022), the Supreme Court held that the Council's recommendations are not binding on the Union and the States, but are the product of collaborative dialogue and have persuasive value, since Article 246A confers simultaneous power on both. In the 56th meeting of the Council, held in September 2025, it recommended rationalisation of the rate structure, to a two-slab system of five and eighteen per cent for most goods and services, with a special rate of forty per cent for a small number of 'sin' and luxury items; the new rates took effect on 22 September 2025, and the compensation cess was phased out for most items. Input tax credit (ITC) is central to the design of GST, which is a destination-based, multi-point tax with credit for tax paid on inputs. In Eicher Motors v. Union of India (1999), the Court held that credit once earned is a vested right, but in Jayam and Co. v. Assistant Commissioner (2016), it held that credit is a concession and not a vested right, available only in accordance with the conditions of the statute. In Safari Retreats Pvt. Ltd. v. Chief Commissioner of CGST (2024), the Court interpreted the expression 'plant or machinery' in Section 17(5)(d) of the CGST Act by the 'functionality test', so that a building may qualify as plant where it is used as a tool for rendering the taxable service, as distinct from the mere premises.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following correctly describes the voting structure of the GST Council as per the passage?","o":["Simple majority, with the Centre holding two-thirds of the weight","Two-thirds majority, with the Centre and States having equal weight","Three-fourths of weighted votes, the Centre holding one-third and the States two-thirds","Unanimity of all members present and voting"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Mohit Minerals, the Supreme Court held that the GST Council's recommendations are","o":["binding only on the States, but not on the Union","binding only on the Union, but not on the States","binding on both the Union and the States","persuasive, and not binding on the Union and the States"],"a":3,"e":"This is the holding as stated in the passage.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following is correct regarding the September 2025 rationalisation?","o":["It increased all rates to twenty-eight per cent for most goods and services and added a cess on every item","It created a two-slab structure of five and eighteen per cent, with forty per cent for sin and luxury items","It created a single uniform rate for all goods and services","It eliminated GST on all goods and replaced it with a compensation cess"],"a":1,"e":"The passage describes the new slab structure and effective date.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The apparent difference between Eicher Motors and Jayam and Co. concerns whether input tax credit is","o":["applicable chiefly to services, and not to the supply of goods","a vested right or a statutory concession subject to conditions","taxable in the hands of the recipient of the supply","available mainly to exporters of goods and services"],"a":1,"e":"Eicher held credit to be a vested right; Jayam held it to be a concession.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under the 'functionality test' in Safari Retreats, a building may be 'plant' where","o":["it is above a certain height or number of storeys","it is used as a tool for rendering the taxable service, and not as mere premises","it is used merely as premises for the supplier's business, and is let out to others for rent","it is owned by the Government or a public authority"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the test.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The best reason why the Court held GST Council recommendations not binding is that","o":["the Council is not a constitutional body, and so has no recommendatory power under the Constitution","the Council has no power to make recommendations on rates unless the Centre agrees","Article 246A gives both the Union and States simultaneous power, and federalism is collaborative","Article 279A, which creates the Council, is itself invalid"],"a":2,"e":"The Court relied on the concurrent power and collaborative dialogue.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P11","s":"Competition Law","sf":"Competition Law","t":"Anti-competitive Conduct, Dominance and the 2023 Amendments","x":"The Competition Act, 2002 prohibits three categories of conduct that harm competition: anti-competitive agreements under Section 3, abuse of dominant position under Section 4, and combinations that cause or are likely to cause an appreciable adverse effect on competition under Sections 5 and 6. Under Section 3(3), horizontal agreements between competitors on prices, output, markets or bid rigging are presumed to have an appreciable adverse effect on competition (AAEC), and the burden is on the parties to rebut the presumption. Vertical agreements, such as tie-in arrangements, exclusive supply and resale price maintenance, are judged under Section 3(4) by a rule-of-reason analysis, which weighs the pro-competitive and anti-competitive effects. Dominance, under Section 4, is not itself an offence; abuse of dominance, including imposing unfair or discriminatory conditions, limiting production or denying market access, is. In Competition Commission of India v. Coordination Committee of Artists and Technicians (2017), the Supreme Court held that trade associations can be liable under Section 3. In Excel Crop Care v. Competition Commission of India (2017), the Court held that the penalty under Section 27 should be calculated on the 'relevant turnover', that is, the turnover from the products affected by the contravention, and not on total turnover, in line with the principle of proportionality. The Competition (Amendment) Act, 2023 changed this by enabling penalties to be based on the 'global turnover' of the enterprise, introduced a deal value threshold of Rs 2,000 crore for review of combinations for transactions where the target has substantial business operations in India, introduced settlements and commitments for vertical agreements and abuse of dominance cases, and provided a 'hub and spoke' cartel concept where an enterprise not engaged in identical activity facilitates a cartel. The Commission's order against Google in the Android case (2022), imposing a penalty of about Rs 1,337 crore for abuse of dominance in the mobile operating system market, was largely upheld by the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal, and an appeal is before the Supreme Court.","q":[{"q":"Under Section 3(3), horizontal agreements on prices or bid rigging are","o":["presumed to have an appreciable adverse effect, with the burden on the parties to rebut","lawful unless the Commission proves an appreciable adverse effect on competition","judged under a rule of reason that weighs pro- and anti-competitive effects","exempt from scrutiny where the parties are enterprises with small market shares"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the rebuttable presumption.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is correct about dominance under Section 4?","o":["Holding a dominant position is generally an offence under Section 4","Dominance is irrelevant to the Act, which targets agreements","Government entities and public undertakings are generally the ones that can be dominant","Dominance is not an offence in itself; its abuse is prohibited"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that dominance is not itself an offence.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Excel Crop Care, the Supreme Court held that penalty should be calculated on","o":["the assets of the enterprise, as shown in its balance sheet","the profits of the enterprise in the financial year of the contravention","the total turnover of the enterprise from all products","relevant turnover, being turnover from the affected products"],"a":3,"e":"The Court applied proportionality and relevant turnover; the 2023 Amendment now permits global turnover.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The 2023 Amendment, in relation to the penalty, changed the position by","o":["fixing it at a maximum of Rs 1 crore for every contravention","allowing it to be computed on the enterprise's global turnover","confirming relevant turnover as the usual basis","abolishing penalties for first-time contraventions"],"a":1,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A company's acquisition involves a deal value of Rs 2,500 crore and the target has substantial business operations in India, though neither party meets the asset or turnover thresholds. Under the 2023 Amendment, as described,","o":["the transaction is void, as neither party meets the thresholds, unless exempted","no review is needed, since the asset and turnover thresholds are unmet","the deal value threshold of Rs 2,000 crore may bring it under review","the Supreme Court must approve the combination before it can proceed"],"a":2,"e":"The passage describes the deal value threshold.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Which of the following is correct about the 'hub and spoke' concept?","o":["It has been abolished and replaced by settlements and commitments","It applies chiefly to competitors engaged in identical activity","It applies chiefly to cartels formed in government procurement","It covers a non-identical enterprise that facilitates a cartel"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the Amendment covers a facilitating enterprise.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P12","s":"Consumer Protection Law","sf":"Consumer Protection Law","t":"Consumers, Product Liability and Dark Patterns","x":"The Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (CPA) replaced the 1986 Act and widened the scope of consumer protection. A 'consumer' is a person who buys goods or hires or avails of services for consideration, but not one who obtains them for resale or for any commercial purpose, though the Act clarifies that 'commercial purpose' does not include use by a person exclusively for earning livelihood by means of self-employment. In Lucknow Development Authority v. M.K. Gupta (1994), the Supreme Court held that housing construction and development activity by a statutory body is a 'service', and in Indian Medical Association v. V.P. Shantha (1995) the Court held that medical services, other than free service rendered to all patients, are covered. In Laxmi Engineering Works v. P.S.G. Industrial Institute (1995), the Court explained that purchase of goods for use in a commercial activity is excluded, subject to the exception for self-employment. The Act creates the Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA), with power to regulate matters relating to violation of consumer rights, unfair trade practices and false or misleading advertisements, to order recall of goods, and to impose penalties on manufacturers and endorsers. Chapter VI introduces 'product liability', making the manufacturer, service provider and seller liable for harm caused by a defective product or deficient service, subject to the defences specified. The Act also deals with 'unfair contracts', defined to include contracts which cause significant change in the rights of consumers, such as excessive security deposits or unilateral termination. In Ireo Grace Realtech v. Abhishek Khanna (2021), the Supreme Court held that one-sided clauses in a builder-buyer agreement, such as those allowing the builder to delay possession but making the buyer liable to heavy interest for delay, amount to an unfair trade practice. The Guidelines for Prevention and Regulation of Dark Patterns, 2023, identify practices such as false urgency, basket sneaking, confirm shaming, forced action and subscription traps as deceptive designs used by online platforms, which are regarded as unfair trade practices.","q":[{"q":"Which of the following is correctly stated about 'consumer' under the 2019 Act?","o":["A person buying goods for resale is a consumer if the goods are bought for consideration","Commercial use excludes a buyer, except use solely for self-employment livelihood","Any buyer of goods is a consumer, even where the purpose is commercial","A person who obtains a service free of charge is a consumer under the Act"],"a":1,"e":"The passage sets out the definition and exception.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Supreme Court in M.K. Gupta held that housing construction by a statutory body is","o":["exempt from the Act since it is a sovereign function","a 'service' covered by the consumer protection law","a sale of goods within the meaning of the Act","not a service, as it is a one-time development activity"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states this.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Medical services are covered by the Act, according to the decision in V.P. Shantha, except","o":["services rendered by private doctors and nursing homes","emergency services rendered in government hospitals","services rendered free of charge to all patients","services provided in hospitals run by charitable trusts"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states the exception.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is a power of the CCPA described in the passage?","o":["To adjudicate individual consumer disputes of any pecuniary value","To order the recall of goods and penalise manufacturers and endorsers","To try criminal offences of cheating arising out of consumer complaints, subject to the Code","To amend the provisions of the Act through its own regulations"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states these powers.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"An online platform shows a banner 'Only 1 left! 10 people viewing' falsely, to hasten the consumer's purchase. This is an example of","o":["a dark pattern, namely false urgency, treated as an unfair trade practice","a product liability claim against the seller on account of a defect","may be a legitimate marketing strategy, provided the goods are actually on sale","a defective service for which the platform must refund the price"],"a":0,"e":"The passage lists false urgency as a dark pattern.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"In Ireo Grace Realtech, the Supreme Court held that a clause allowing the builder to delay possession while imposing heavy interest on the buyer for delay is","o":["valid, being a matter of contractual freedom between the parties","an unfair trade practice, since the clause is one-sided against the buyer","a matter generally for civil courts, not consumer fora","exempt from scrutiny as it forms part of a commercial contract"],"a":1,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P13","s":"Law of Contract","sf":"Law of Contract / Specific Relief","t":"Specific Performance after the 2018 Amendment","x":"The Specific Relief Act, 1963 was amended in 2018 to alter the character of specific performance in India. Before the amendment, Section 10 provided that specific performance of a contract could be enforced in the discretion of the court, which was guided by Section 20; the remedy of damages was the primary relief, and specific performance an exception. After the amendment, Section 10 provides that specific performance of a contract shall be enforced by the court, subject to Sections 11(2), 14 and 16; the discretion has been removed, and the remedy has become the rule. Section 14 sets out the contracts not specifically enforceable, including those where compensation in money is an adequate relief, contracts which run into minute details or are dependent on the personal qualifications of the parties, contracts which are in their nature determinable, and contracts the performance of which involves the continuous duty which the court cannot supervise, subject to modifications introduced in 2018 for certain contracts for infrastructure projects. Section 16 bars relief to a person who has become incapable of performing, has violated an essential term, or who fails to aver and prove that he has performed or has always been 'ready and willing' to perform the essential terms of the contract, other than those which have been prevented or waived by the defendant. The amendment also introduced Section 20, which enables the party suffering from breach to obtain substituted performance by a third party or through his own agency at the cost of the defaulting party, after giving a written notice of thirty days; once this remedy is chosen, the party cannot claim specific performance. Section 20A bars the grant of an injunction by a court in suits involving a contract relating to an infrastructure project, where the grant would cause impediment or delay in the progress or completion of the project, and Section 20B provides for designated special courts. In Katta Sujatha Reddy v. Siddamsetty Infra Projects (2024), the Supreme Court held that the 2018 amendments are substantive in nature and apply prospectively to contracts entered into after 1 October 2018. Section 21 allows damages, with or without specific performance, and Section 38 provides for a perpetual injunction to prevent the breach of an obligation.","q":[{"q":"After the 2018 Amendment, specific performance under Section 10 is","o":["abolished and replaced by compensation in most cases","available chiefly for contracts relating to immovable property","now the rule, enforceable subject to Sections 11(2), 14 and 16","a discretionary remedy, as it was before the amendment"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states that the discretion was removed.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following contracts is NOT specifically enforceable under Section 14 as described?","o":["Contracts that are in their nature determinable by either party","Contracts for the sale of land where the buyer is ready and willing","Contracts where compensation in money is an adequate relief","Contracts dependent on the personal qualifications of the parties"],"a":1,"e":"The last is enforceable if the buyer is ready and willing.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under Section 20 as introduced, the party who gets the contract performed by a third party","o":["cannot afterwards claim specific performance of the same contract","may still claim specific performance of the contract as well","must first file a suit and obtain a decree for substituted performance","has no right to recover the costs from the defaulting party"],"a":0,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The purpose of Section 20A is to","o":["bar injunctions that would impede or delay infrastructure projects","make the grant of injunctions in infrastructure disputes automatic","compel completion of infrastructure projects even at the expense of other parties","invalidate infrastructure contracts that cause delay in completion"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the purpose.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A contract was entered into on 1 June 2017, and a suit was filed in 2020. On the Supreme Court's decision in Katta Sujatha Reddy, the amended Section 10","o":["applies chiefly to infrastructure contracts, not to other contracts","applies, as the suit was instituted after the amendment came into force","applies retrospectively to contracts made before 2018 as well","does not apply, as it covers only contracts made after 1 October 2018"],"a":3,"e":"The date of the contract is determinative.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Consider the following. 1. The 2018 amendment removed the court's discretion in Section 10. 2. Section 16 requires the plaintiff to prove readiness and willingness. 3. Substituted performance requires a notice of thirty days. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"e":"All three statements are made in the passage.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P14","s":"Law of Torts","sf":"Law of Torts / Motor Accident Claims","t":"Compensation for Death, Future Prospects and the Value of Homemaking","x":"Compensation in motor accident claims is governed by the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988. Section 166 enables a claim for compensation to be made before the Motor Accidents Claims Tribunal, and the principle is of 'just compensation', and not a mere formula. Section 140 provides for no-fault liability, a fixed sum for death and for grievous hurt, without proof of negligence. In Sarla Verma v. Delhi Transport Corporation (2009), the Supreme Court standardised the calculation in death cases: the income of the deceased is established, a deduction is made for personal expenses of the deceased, one-third where there are two or three dependants, and so on, and a multiplier is selected from a table according to the age of the deceased. In National Insurance Co. Ltd. v. Pranay Sethi (2017), a Constitution Bench held that future prospects must be added to the income of the deceased, even where he was in permanent employment or self-employed with a fixed salary, at forty per cent where the deceased was below forty years, twenty-five per cent where between forty and fifty, and ten per cent where between fifty and sixty; it also fixed conventional sums for loss of estate, funeral expenses and loss of consortium, with a ten per cent increase every three years. In respect of homemakers, the Court in Arun Kumar Agrawal v. National Insurance Co. (2010) held that the services of a homemaker, though not performed for pay, are of economic value, and in Kirti v. Oriental Insurance Co. (2021) the Court held that the notional income assigned to a homemaker should reflect the value of the work done and should not be treated as a token, since to do so would ignore the contribution of women to the household and the economy. In Shishu Pal v. Surjeet, decided on 11 June 2026, a Bench of Justices Sanjay Karol and N. Kotiswar Singh, in a motor accident compensation case, stressed the economic and social contribution of homemakers, and treated it as an element to be reflected in compensation. The Court has repeatedly said that compensation should aim at restoring the dependants to the position they would have been in had the death not occurred, as far as money can do so.","q":[{"q":"Section 140 of the Motor Vehicles Act provides for","o":["no-fault liability, a fixed sum for death or grievous hurt","compulsory insurance of all pedestrians injured on the road","compensation payable after the claimant proves negligence","no compensation for self-employed persons or homemakers"],"a":0,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Under Pranay Sethi, future prospects for a deceased aged forty-five years are added at","o":["forty per cent","ten per cent","fifteen per cent","twenty-five per cent"],"a":3,"e":"Between forty and fifty years the addition is twenty-five per cent.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Sarla Verma, a multiplier was chosen according to","o":["the type of vehicle involved in the accident","the income of the deceased at the time of death","the number of dependants left by the deceased","the age of the deceased, as shown in a table"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the multiplier is chosen by age.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The Court's reasoning on the notional income of homemakers in Kirti was that it should","o":["reflect the value of the work done and not be a mere token","be fixed at a uniform figure for all homemakers across the country","be treated as nil, because a homemaker receives no wages for the work","be pegged to the statutory minimum wage of the State concerned"],"a":0,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A forty-year-old self-employed person with a fixed income dies in a road accident. According to Pranay Sethi, future prospects","o":["are not added because he was self-employed rather than a salaried employee","are added at forty per cent because he was below forty-one years","are decided by the insurance company, subject to the Tribunal's approval","are added at the rate for his age bracket, even for the self-employed"],"a":3,"e":"The Constitution Bench held that future prospects apply to the self-employed with a fixed salary as well. (A person aged exactly forty falls into the forty-to-fifty bracket, namely twenty-five per cent.)","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Which of the following describes the principle of 'just compensation' in the passage?","o":["Compensation measured by the degree of negligence of the driver and the vehicle owner","Restoring the dependants, so far as money can, to their earlier position","The highest compensation the Tribunal can award in a given case","A fixed sum payable on death, with a modest adjustment for income"],"a":1,"e":"This is stated at the end of the passage.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P15","s":"Administrative Law","sf":"Administrative Law","t":"The Right to Information: Public Authority, Exemptions and Public Interest","x":"The Right to Information Act, 2005 gives effect to the right to information under Article 19(1)(a). Every citizen has the right to information held by or under the control of a 'public authority', which is defined in Section 2(h) to include bodies established by the Constitution or by law, and bodies owned, controlled or substantially financed by the appropriate Government. In Thalappalam Service Cooperative Bank v. State of Kerala (2013), the Supreme Court held that cooperative societies are not public authorities merely because they are registered under a State Act, unless they are substantially financed or controlled by the Government, and that 'substantial' financing must be a major part of the funding, not a mere token amount. The Public Information Officer must dispose of a request within thirty days, or forty-eight hours where the information concerns the life or liberty of a person (Section 7(1)). Section 8(1) contains exemptions, including information affecting sovereignty and security, information forbidden to be published by a court, information whose disclosure would endanger life, information held in a fiduciary relationship, and personal information which has no relationship to any public activity or interest or which would cause an unwarranted invasion of privacy (Section 8(1)(j)). Section 8(2) provides that, notwithstanding the exemptions, access may be allowed if the public interest in disclosure outweighs the harm to the protected interest. In Girish Ramchandra Deshpande v. Central Information Commissioner (2013), the Court held that service records and assessment of an employee are personal information, exempt unless a larger public interest is shown. In Reserve Bank of India v. Jayantilal N. Mistry (2015), the Court held that the regulator cannot rely on a 'fiduciary' relationship with banks to refuse information on inspection reports, since the regulator does not hold the information for the benefit of banks. In Central Public Information Officer, Supreme Court of India v. Subhash Chandra Agarwal (2019), a Constitution Bench held that the office of the Chief Justice of India is a public authority, that the right to information and judicial independence are not antagonistic, and that the balance between privacy and transparency is to be struck by applying the proportionality test and by considering the public interest in each case. Section 24 exempts specified intelligence and security organisations, except for allegations of corruption and human rights violations.","q":[{"q":"Under Thalappalam, a cooperative society is a public authority only if it is","o":["a cooperative bank, whatever its source of finance","registered under a State cooperative societies Act","established before the Constitution came into force","substantially financed or controlled by the Government"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states the test.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The time limit for disposal of a request concerning the life or liberty of a person is","o":["ninety days from the date of the request","thirty days from the date of the request","forty-eight hours from the request","seven days from the date of the request"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"In Jayantilal Mistry, the Supreme Court held that the RBI","o":["could not claim a fiduciary relationship, as it holds no information for the banks' benefit","could withhold inspection reports, because it holds them in a fiduciary relationship","was not a public authority, as it is an autonomous statutory regulator","was exempt in full under Section 24 as an intelligence and security body"],"a":0,"e":"The passage records the reasoning.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"What did the Constitution Bench hold in the Subhash Chandra Agarwal case regarding the Office of the Chief Justice of India?","o":["It is a public authority, and transparency is compatible with judicial independence","It is exempt under Section 24, since judicial independence demands secrecy","It is not a public authority, since judges are constitutional functionaries and not employees","Serving judges and court officers may seek information from it"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the holding.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"A citizen asks for the assessment record of a public servant. According to Girish Deshpande, the request would","o":["be heard by a court, as the Commission lacks jurisdiction over service matters","be refused as personal information, unless a larger public interest is shown","be allowed as a matter of right, as the person is a public servant","be allowed if the citizen pays the prescribed fee for the record"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held service records to be personal information exempt unless larger public interest.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The role of Section 8(2) as described is that it","o":["fixes the time limit for disposal of requests by the PIO","adds new exemptions for sensitive categories of information","allows disclosure despite exemptions if public interest outweighs the harm","exempts intelligence and security organisations from the Act"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states the override.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P16","s":"Civil Procedure and Commercial Disputes","sf":"Civil Procedure and Commercial Disputes","t":"Pleadings, Pre-institution Mediation and Summary Judgment","x":"The Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 provides, in Section 11, that no court shall try a suit or issue in which the matter directly and substantially in issue has been directly and substantially in issue in a former suit between the same parties, or parties under whom they claim, where the former court was competent to try the later suit and decided it finally. Order VII Rule 11 permits the rejection of a plaint where it does not disclose a cause of action, is under-valued, is insufficiently stamped, or is barred by law. In Dahiben v. Arvindbhai Kalyanji Bhanusali (2020), the Supreme Court held that, in considering an application under Order VII Rule 11, the court must examine only the plaint and the documents filed with it, and not the defence of the defendant, and that the application cannot be used to decide disputed questions of fact. The Commercial Courts Act, 2015 introduced a special procedure for 'commercial disputes' of the specified value. Section 12A makes pre-institution mediation mandatory for suits that do not contemplate urgent interim relief; in Patil Automation Pvt. Ltd. v. Rakheja Engineers Pvt. Ltd. (2022), the Supreme Court held that the requirement is mandatory, and that a plaint instituted in non-compliance is liable to be rejected under Order VII Rule 11, but applied the ruling prospectively from 20 August 2022. In SCG Contracts (India) Pvt. Ltd. v. K.S. Chamankar Infrastructure Pvt. Ltd. (2019), the Court held that the period for filing a written statement in a commercial suit, thirty days, extendable by not more than ninety days, is strictly mandatory, and that the court has no power to extend it further. Order XIII-A, inserted by the Commercial Courts Act, enables the court to give summary judgment on a claim where it considers that the claimant has no real prospect of succeeding or that the defendant has no real prospect of successfully defending the claim, and there is no other compelling reason why the claim should not be disposed of before trial. The Mediation Act, 2023 gives statutory form to mediation, provides for community mediation, and makes a mediated settlement agreement enforceable in the same manner as a judgment or decree of a court.","q":[{"q":"In an application under Order VII Rule 11, the court examines","o":["the plaint and the written statement filed by the defendant","the documents and pleas produced by the defendant","only the plaint and the documents filed along with it","the evidence of both parties led at a preliminary hearing"],"a":2,"e":"Dahiben held that the defence is not to be considered.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to Patil Automation, pre-institution mediation under Section 12A is","o":["mandatory only for commercial suits where the value exceeds Rs 10 crore","required only where the commercial dispute involves a public authority","optional, with non-compliance treated as a curable procedural irregularity","mandatory, so a non-compliant plaint may be rejected, applying from 20 August 2022"],"a":3,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A suit for urgent interim relief is filed in a commercial court without pre-institution mediation. Under Section 12A as described, the suit","o":["must be rejected at the threshold, as mediation is mandatory in commercial suits generally","is not barred, as Section 12A excludes suits contemplating urgent interim relief","must be transferred to the mediation authority for settlement first","is barred under Order VII Rule 11 unless mediation is completed within sixty days"],"a":1,"e":"The passage says the section applies to suits that do not contemplate urgent interim relief.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"The maximum period for filing a written statement in a commercial suit under SCG Contracts is","o":["one hundred and eighty days, if the court is satisfied about the delay","one hundred and twenty days, with no power to extend it further","thirty days, with no extension in a commercial suit","ninety days in all, extendable by costs imposed on the defendant"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the thirty-plus-ninety rule as mandatory.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Summary judgment under Order XIII-A may be passed where","o":["the plaintiff files an application and the defendant does not object to it","the defendant is absent and no written statement has been filed","a party has no real prospect of success and there is no compelling reason for trial","the claim exceeds one crore and rests wholly on documentary evidence"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states the test.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under the Mediation Act, 2023, a mediated settlement agreement is","o":["enforceable by way of a separate arbitration proceeding","enforceable in the same manner as a judgment or decree of a court","enforceable if registered with the Registrar of the High Court","a mere contract, enforceable only through a fresh civil suit"],"a":1,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Easy"}]},{"id":"P17","s":"Family Law","sf":"Family Law / Succession","t":"Daughters' Coparcenary Rights and the Hindu Succession Act","x":"Before 2005, the Mitakshara coparcenary consisted only of male members, and a daughter was not a coparcener. The Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005, which substituted Section 6 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, provides that on and from 9 September 2005, the daughter of a coparcener shall, by birth, become a coparcener in her own right in the same manner as the son, with the same rights and liabilities, and may dispose of her share by testamentary disposition. The question whether the daughter's right depended on the father being alive on that date led to conflicting views. In Prakash v. Phulavati (2016), the Court held that the amendment was prospective and required that both the daughter and the father be alive on 9 September 2005, while Danamma v. Amar (2018) took a contrary view. In Vineeta Sharma v. Rakesh Sharma (2020), a three-judge Bench settled the law, holding that the coparcenary right accrues by birth and is not dependent upon the father being alive on 9 September 2005, since the right is by birth, though the provision is not retroactive in the sense that it does not disturb partitions or dispositions effected before 20 December 2004, which must be proved by a registered partition deed or a decree of court and not by an oral partition, except in exceptional cases. Section 14 provides that any property possessed by a female Hindu is held as her absolute property, and in V. Tulasamma v. Sesha Reddy (1977) the Court held that a limited estate in lieu of maintenance matured into an absolute estate. In Arunachala Gounder v. Ponnusamy (2022), the Court held that the self-acquired property of a Hindu male dying intestate, even prior to 1956, would devolve on his daughter by inheritance and not by survivorship. Under Section 15, the property of a female dying intestate devolves first upon her sons and daughters and the husband, then upon the heirs of the husband, and so on; Section 15(2) provides that property inherited from her father or mother goes, in the absence of children, to the heirs of the father, and property inherited from her husband or father-in-law goes to the heirs of the husband. In Revanasiddappa v. Mallikarjun (2023), a Bench held that children of void and voidable marriages, who are legitimate under Section 16 of the Hindu Marriage Act, are entitled to the share in the parent's property, including the coparcenary property.","q":[{"q":"According to Vineeta Sharma, the daughter's coparcenary right under the amended Section 6","o":["applies to daughters who were born after 9 September 2005","requires the coparcener father to be alive on 9 September 2005","accrues by birth, whether or not the father was alive on 9 September 2005","applies to the father's self-acquired property, and not to joint property"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under Vineeta Sharma, a partition effected before 20 December 2004 must be proved by","o":["a notarised affidavit executed by all coparceners and attested by two witnesses","a registered partition deed or a court decree, save in exceptional cases","evidence of neighbours and the revenue records of the village","an oral partition proved by the statements of the coparceners"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the requirement.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct statement regarding Prakash v. Phulavati?","o":["Its father-alive requirement was not accepted in Vineeta Sharma","It was approved and generally followed by the three-judge Bench in Vineeta Sharma","It conferred coparcenary rights on sons, not on daughters","It abolished the Mitakshara coparcenary for all Hindu families"],"a":0,"e":"Vineeta Sharma settled the conflict contrary to Prakash.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"A Hindu woman dies intestate leaving no children, husband or her husband's heirs. She had inherited the property from her father. Under Section 15(2), the property goes to","o":["the heirs of her mother, as the nearest blood relatives","the heirs of her husband, as the family she married into","the heirs of her father, from whom she inherited it","the Government, by escheat, as no heirs were left"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 15(2), property inherited from the father goes back to the heirs of the father in the absence of children.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Arunachala Gounder held that the self-acquired property of a Hindu male dying intestate, even before 1956, devolves on his daughter","o":["by escheat","by will","by inheritance","by survivorship"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states 'by inheritance and not by survivorship'.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Children of void and voidable marriages under Revanasiddappa are entitled to","o":["maintenance from the parent's estate, and not a share","a share in the parent's property, including coparcenary property","a share only in the mother's separate property, not coparcenary","no share in the property of their parents, which passes to legitimate children"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the holding.","d":"Moderate"}]},{"id":"P18","s":"Public International Law","sf":"Public International Law","t":"Sources of International Law, Jus Cogens and Obligations Erga Omnes","x":"Article 38(1) of the Statute of the International Court of Justice lists the sources the Court applies: international conventions establishing rules expressly recognised by the contesting States; international custom, as evidence of a general practice accepted as law; the general principles of law recognised by civilised nations; and, as subsidiary means for the determination of rules of law, judicial decisions and the teachings of the most highly qualified publicists. Customary international law requires two elements: settled state practice, which must be extensive and virtually uniform, and opinio juris, the belief that the practice is required by law. In the North Sea Continental Shelf Cases (1969), the Court held that a treaty provision may generate a customary rule, but only where it is of a fundamentally norm-creating character and has been accepted in practice, and that the passage of a short period of time is not a bar. In the Asylum Case (Colombia v. Peru) (1950), the Court held that a party relying on a regional or local custom must prove that it is constant and uniform. A State that persistently objects to an emerging custom while it is being formed is not bound by it, the 'persistent objector' rule. In Nicaragua v. United States (1986), the Court held that customary rules continue to exist alongside treaty rules with identical content and retain an independent existence. Peremptory norms, or jus cogens, are defined in Article 53 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties as norms accepted and recognised by the international community as a whole from which no derogation is permitted, and which can be modified only by a subsequent norm of the same character; a treaty in conflict with such a norm is void. In Barcelona Traction (1970), the Court distinguished obligations owed to the international community as a whole, or erga omnes, such as the prohibition of aggression and genocide, in whose protection all States have a legal interest. In the proceedings brought by South Africa against Israel under the Genocide Convention, the Court in January 2024 held that it had prima facie jurisdiction, that the rights claimed were plausible, and indicated provisional measures; the case rests on the idea that obligations under the Convention are owed erga omnes partes. Under Article 59 of the Statute, a decision of the Court has no binding force except between the parties and in respect of that particular case.","q":[{"q":"The two elements of customary international law are","o":["treaty and consent of States","judicial precedent and equity","state practice and opinio juris","legislation and global comity"],"a":2,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"A State consistently objects to an emerging rule of custom during its formation. Under the 'persistent objector' rule it","o":["is not bound by the rule in respect of that State","is bound by the rule, like all other States","must ratify a treaty to remain outside the rule","may lose its seat in the UN General Assembly"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the rule.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"According to Nicaragua v. United States, where a treaty and a customary rule have identical content,","o":["the customary rule is generally absorbed into the treaty and ceases to exist","the Court cannot apply either rule in the absence of consent","the treaty is void to the extent that it duplicates custom","the customary rule continues to exist independently of the treaty"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states this.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct statement concerning jus cogens?","o":["A peremptory norm can be modified by a later treaty between States","Peremptory norms bind those States that are parties to the Statute","A treaty in conflict with a peremptory norm is voidable, not void","A conflicting treaty is void; only a norm of equal character can modify it"],"a":3,"e":"Article 53 as set out in the passage.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The distinction between obligations erga omnes and ordinary obligations lies in the fact that erga omnes obligations","o":["are non-binding on States that have not expressly consented to them","apply during armed conflict, and not in peacetime relations","are owed to the international community, so every State has a legal interest","are owed to the victim State, which alone may invoke responsibility for the breach"],"a":2,"e":"The passage describes Barcelona Traction.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Consider the following. 1. A judicial decision of the ICJ binds only the parties in respect of the particular case. 2. Judicial decisions are a primary source under Article 38(1). 3. Opinio juris is a necessary element of custom. Which are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"e":"Judicial decisions are subsidiary means under Article 38(1)(d), so statement 2 is wrong.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P19","s":"Public International Law","sf":"Public International Law","t":"The International Criminal Court: Jurisdiction, Immunities and Cooperation","x":"The Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court (ICC), adopted in 1998 and in force since 2002, establishes a permanent court with jurisdiction over genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and the crime of aggression, the last being activated after the Kampala amendments of 2010 on conditions narrower than those for other crimes. The Court is complementary to national criminal jurisdictions: under Article 17, a case is inadmissible where it is being investigated or prosecuted by a State with jurisdiction, unless the State is unwilling or unable genuinely to do so. Under Article 12, the Court may exercise jurisdiction where the crime was committed on the territory of a State Party or by a national of a State Party, or where a non-party State has accepted the jurisdiction by declaration; under Article 13(b), the Security Council, acting under Chapter VII, may refer a situation to the Prosecutor, in which case the Court's jurisdiction extends to non-parties. Under Article 16, the Security Council may defer an investigation or prosecution for a renewable period of twelve months. Article 27 provides that the Statute applies equally to all persons without distinction based on official capacity, and that immunities attaching to the official capacity of a person, whether under national or international law, do not bar the Court's jurisdiction. Article 98, however, provides that the Court may not proceed with a request for surrender which would require a State to act inconsistently with its obligations under international law with respect to the immunities of a third State, unless that State's cooperation for waiver is obtained. In the Jordan Referral decision (2019), concerning the failure of Jordan to arrest Sudan's President Omar al-Bashir, the Appeals Chamber held that there is no rule of customary international law recognising head-of-state immunity before an international court, and that States Parties are bound to cooperate. The Court has issued arrest warrants against sitting leaders, including those against the Russian President in March 2023, and against the Israeli Prime Minister and a former Defence Minister in November 2024, which provoked debate on the effect of immunities, and in March 2025, a former Philippine President was surrendered to the Court. India, the United States and China are not parties to the Rome Statute.","q":[{"q":"The principle of complementarity under Article 17 means that a case is inadmissible where","o":["the accused is a sitting head of State or other senior official of a State","the Security Council has deferred the investigation for twelve months","the crime was committed on the territory of a State that is not a party","a State with jurisdiction genuinely investigates, unless it is unwilling or unable"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states the principle.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is a route through which the ICC may exercise jurisdiction over a national of a non-party State?","o":["A declaration by the accused person accepting jurisdiction","A referral by the Security Council acting under Article 13(b)","A request from any State, whether or not it is a party","A referral by the General Assembly by a two-thirds vote"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that a Security Council referral extends jurisdiction to non-parties.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Under Article 16, the Security Council may","o":["defer an investigation or prosecution for renewable twelve-month periods","permanently veto an arrest warrant issued by the Pre-Trial Chamber","terminate the Court's proceedings against a non-party national","nominate the Prosecutor of the Court from a list of candidates"],"a":0,"e":"The passage so states.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"The apparent tension between Articles 27 and 98 is that","o":["both articles apply to the crime of aggression, which follows a separate jurisdictional regime","Article 27 removes immunity before the Court, while Article 98 limits surrender involving third-State immunities","there is no tension, because Article 98 applies after conviction and sentence by the Court","Article 98 overrides Article 27, so that official immunities now bar every ICC prosecution, even of a sitting head of State"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states both provisions.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"In the Jordan Referral decision, the Appeals Chamber held that","o":["no customary rule recognises head-of-state immunity before an international court","only the Security Council may order the arrest of a sitting head of State","Jordan was not bound by the Statute, since the referral was by a non-party","head-of-state immunity bars the ICC from proceeding against Sudan's sitting President"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the holding.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is correct?","o":["India is a party to the Rome Statute and bound by its Article 98","The crime of aggression stands on the same footing as other crimes under the Rome Statute","The ICC has no jurisdiction over war crimes, as it covers genocide","India is not a party, though it may be affected by a Security Council referral"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states India is not a party; Security Council referral extends jurisdiction to non-parties.","d":"Tough"}]},{"id":"P20","s":"Intellectual Property Law","sf":"Intellectual Property Law","t":"Trade Marks, Well-known Marks and Pharmaceutical Patents","x":"The Trade Marks Act, 1999 provides for the registration of marks capable of being represented graphically and distinguishing the goods or services of one person from those of others. Section 9 lists absolute grounds for refusal, such as marks devoid of distinctive character, and Section 11 relative grounds, including similarity to an earlier mark and the likelihood of confusion. Under Section 11(6), the Registrar must take into account factors in determining whether a mark is well known, including its knowledge in the relevant section of the public and its duration and extent of use. Section 29(4) protects a mark with a reputation against use of an identical or similar mark in relation to dissimilar goods where the use takes unfair advantage of, or is detrimental to, the mark's distinctive character or repute, a form of dilution. Where the mark is unregistered, the proprietor may bring an action for passing off, the elements of which are goodwill, misrepresentation and damage. In Amritdhara Pharmacy v. Satya Deo Gupta (1963), the Supreme Court said that the test of deceptive similarity is to be applied from the perspective of a person of average intelligence and imperfect recollection, and that the marks are to be compared as a whole. In Cadila Health Care v. Cadila Pharmaceuticals (2001), the Court held that stricter standards apply in cases of medicinal products, since the consequences of confusion between drugs may be harmful. In Toyota Jidosha Kabushiki Kaisha v. Prius Auto Industries (2018), the Court held that a mark's trans-border reputation may be protected even where it is not used in India, but that the claimant must show that the reputation had spilled over to India at the relevant time. The Patents Act, 1970 requires novelty, an inventive step and industrial applicability; Section 3(d) excludes the mere discovery of a new form of a known substance unless it results in enhanced efficacy, a provision explained in Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013), where the Supreme Court denied a patent to the beta crystalline form of imatinib mesylate. Section 84 permits the grant of a compulsory licence where the reasonable requirements of the public have not been satisfied, the invention is not available at a reasonably affordable price, or it is not worked in India; in Natco Pharma v. Bayer Corporation (2012), the first compulsory licence was granted for a cancer drug.","q":[{"q":"The test of deceptive similarity in Amritdhara is applied from the viewpoint of","o":["the proprietor of the registered mark, being the aggrieved party","a judge, applying a detailed point-by-point analysis of the marks","a person of average intelligence and imperfect recollection","an expert in trade marks, comparing the marks feature by feature"],"a":2,"e":"The passage states the test.","d":"Easy"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an element of passing off, as stated in the passage?","o":["Prior registration of the mark under the Act","Misrepresentation by the defendant to the public","Goodwill attaching to the claimant's goods or services","Damage, or likelihood of damage, to the claimant"],"a":0,"e":"The passage lists the three elements; registration is not required for passing off.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Cadila Health Care, a stricter standard was applied to medicinal products because","o":["the Act so provides for medicines and pharmaceutical preparations","confusion between two medicines may have harmful consequences","pharmaceutical companies have stronger goodwill than other traders","drugs are costly and are bought by the public regularly"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states the reason.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"In Toyota v. Prius, the claimant's trans-border reputation would be protected where","o":["the mark is famous anywhere in the world, irrespective of any use in India","the mark is not used in India at all, however well known abroad","the mark is registered in several jurisdictions outside India","the reputation had spilled over to India at the relevant time"],"a":3,"e":"The passage states that the claimant must show spill-over.","d":"Tough"},{"q":"Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, as applied in Novartis, excludes","o":["mere new forms of a known substance, unless efficacy is enhanced","all chemical inventions, even where they show a real inventive step","inventions made abroad by foreign applicants without local working","all pharmaceutical inventions, because medicines are a public good"],"a":0,"e":"The passage states the rule and the outcome.","d":"Moderate"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a ground for a compulsory licence under Section 84 as stated in the passage?","o":["The patentee refuses to license a competitor on commercial terms","The reasonable requirements of the public are not satisfied","The invention is not available at a reasonably affordable price","The invention is not worked in the territory of India"],"a":0,"e":"The passage lists the first three grounds only.","d":"Moderate"}]}]};
